-- title:
--   Commute GHalf Sandwich Move Source Eq Split
-- statement:
--   **The move-source outcome is the split head-tail ordered outcome.**
--
--   Let $(m,q,d)$ have a field model, let $F$ be a slice-indexed polynomial family, and let $r\in\mathbb N$. Write a triple question as $q=(x_1,x_2,\mathbf y)$, where $x_1,x_2$ are slice questions and $\mathbf y$ is an $r$-tuple of points. Write a corresponding outcome as $(g_1,g_2,\mathbf g)$, where $g_1,g_2$ are completed slice outcomes and $\mathbf g$ is a completed-outcome tuple. Let $M_r^{\mathrm{src}}$ be the move-source family and let $O_{r+1}$ be the head-tail ordered family.
--
--   The pointwise outcome identity is
--
--   $$
--   M_r^{\mathrm{src}}\!\left((x_1,x_2,\mathbf y);(g_1,g_2,\mathbf g)\right)
--   =O_{r+1}\!\left((x_1,(x_2,\mathbf y));(g_1,(g_2,\mathbf g))\right).
--   $$
--
--   Thus the second slice question and its outcome are inserted at the head of the tail tuple. This converts the source family into the split form used by the successor lift.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.commuteGHalfSandwich_moveSource_eq_split`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
